{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011CdsaijcBFExXuYYzpRH7R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Fo2J3Ygk13N3iDME8NCUdR","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-creator-0/113f7cc5-237e-5d6a-8867-a97fd89c58dd/scratchpad/e6/e6-t08/shadow/exec-ws/analysis.md","content":"# Analysis for e6-t08 (E-A input-class analysis and E-C mechanical demonstration)\n\nAuthored at harness node `resolve-local`. All references are to\n`package/artifacts/merge.py` as provided.\n\n## The code under audit\n\n```python\ndef merge(intervals):\n    \"\"\"Merge integer intervals [start, end] into a canonical form.\"\"\"\n    if not intervals:\n        return []\n    ivs = sorted([list(i) for i in intervals])\n    out = [ivs[0][:]]\n    for s, e in ivs[1:]:\n        if s < out[-1][1]:          # line 8: STRICT comparison\n            out[-1][1] = max(out[-1][1], e)\n        else:\n            out.append([s, e])\n    return out\n```\n\nThe branch the claim's truth turns on is line 8: `s < out[-1][1]` is a\nstrict comparison.\n\n## The claim\n\nFor every in-domain input (<= 6 intervals, integer endpoints in [-10, 10],\nstart <= end), the sorted output of `merge` contains **no** pair i before j\nwith `i.end >= j.start`. Note the claim uses `>=`: equality (touching\nintervals in the output) counts as a violation.\n\n## E-A: input classes on which the claim's truth turns\n\nDerived from the control flow of merge.py, not a generic checklist:\n\n1. **Empty list.** Handled by the `if not intervals` guard on line 3; returns\n   `[]`. No pair exists; the claim holds vacuously on this class.\n2. **Singleton list.** The loop body never runs; output is one interval. No\n   pair exists; claim holds on this class.\n3. **Properly overlapping intervals** (`next.start < prev.end` after\n   sorting). Line 8's condition `s < out[-1][1]` is True; the intervals are\n   merged with `max` extending the end. Claim holds on this class.\n4. **Nested / contained intervals** (e.g. `[0, 5]` containing `[1, 2]`).\n   After sorting, the inner interval's start is < the outer's end, so line 8\n   merges; `max(out[-1][1], e)` correctly keeps the outer end. Claim holds.\n5. **Duplicate positive-width intervals** (`[a, b], [a, b]` with `a < b`).\n   Second copy has `s = a < b = out[-1][1]`, so merged. Claim holds.\n6. **Touching intervals** (`prev.end == next.start`, e.g. `[0,1], [1,2]`).\n   Line 8 tests `s < out[-1][1]` **strictly**: `1 < 1` is False, so the\n   second interval is appended unmerged. The sorted output then contains the\n   pair `([0,1], [1,2])` with `i.end = 1 >= 1 = j.start`. **The claim's\n   truth turns on exactly this class: a `<=` on line 8 would make the claim\n   hold here; the actual `<` makes it fail.**\n7. **Zero-width intervals sharing a point** (e.g. `[3,3], [3,3]` or\n   `[3,3], [3,5]`). `s = 3 < out[-1][1] = 3` is False, so the second is\n   appended; output pair has `i.end = 3 >= 3 = j.start`. A second violating\n   subclass of the same strict-comparison behavior.\n8. **Unsorted input.** Line 5 sorts first, so input order cannot by itself\n   produce or mask a violation; this class reduces to the classes above.\n9. **Negative endpoints.** All comparisons are order comparisons on\n   integers; sign plays no role. Reduces to the classes above (e.g.\n   `[-2,-1], [-1,0]` violates identically to class 6).\n\n## E-C: verdict and mechanical demonstration\n\n**Verdict: VIOLATED.**\n\nViolating input: `[[0, 1], [1, 2]]`.\n\nDomain membership: 2 intervals (<= 6); endpoints 0, 1, 1, 2 are integers in\n[-10, 10]; both intervals satisfy start <= end. In-domain.\n\nTrace of the actual code:\n\n1. `intervals = [[0,1],[1,2]]` is non-empty → guard on line 3 not taken.\n2. Line 5: `ivs = sorted([[0,1],[1,2]]) = [[0,1],[1,2]]`.\n3. Line 6: `out = [[0,1]]`.\n4. Loop iteration with `(s, e) = (1, 2)`: line 8 tests `s < out[-1][1]`,\n   i.e. `1 < 1` → **False** → the else branch executes:\n   `out.append([1, 2])`.\n5. Return value: `[[0, 1], [1, 2]]`.\n\nSorted output: `[[0, 1], [1, 2]]` (already sorted). Take i = `[0, 1]`\n(position 0) before j = `[1, 2]` (position 1): `i.end = 1 >= 1 = j.start`.\nThis is exactly the pair the claim forbids, so the claim is mechanically\nviolated by an in-domain input.\n\n## Scope note\n\nThis analysis and verdict were authored by the same session that read the\npackage, which is admissible for E-A and E-C (no authorship restriction).\nE-B (a systematic search by a party other than the author of this\nprovisional answer) is NOT satisfied by this document and is left to the\nroot-executed independent examiner (`verify-independent`).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5183,"cache_read_input_tokens":35310,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5183},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b71655e9-2d00-4ad5-b389-fcd789437a7b","uuid":"7f64d8e5-a679-41c5-9bc6-96b6cb138e2d","timestamp":"2026-08-09T17:38:41.627Z","request_id":"req_011CdsaifiGYELqgytwYETY2"}
